      "provenance": {
        "notes": "Starter row 38 is the family Bromeliaceae; this entry is for the genus Guzmania, the one the lists name first and the one NC State, RHS and Clemson cover. Other bromeliads (Aechmea, Neoregelia, Vriesea) need their own rows if wanted. Watering is a mapping: all three pages say keep the cup filled and let the mix dry between waterings; the watering tip carries the cup rule, the engine only gets the mix. Light: Clemson says guzmanias prefer moderate light, RHS bright with shade from strong sun, NC State full sun to shade by species; stored bright indirect. Temperature is Clemson's 60 to 85 F; RHS gives a 10 C winter minimum. Toxicity has one source: the ASPCA page for Guzmania musaica (Orange Star, G. lingulata, is also listed non-toxic but its page was not read); NC State, RHS and Clemson make no toxicity statement. Spiny-edged relatives such as Aechmea can scratch. Anchor: baseIntervalDays 7 -> 13 for a 3-litre reference pot, from measured water use (Guzmania 'Indian Night', 28.0 g a day); the conversion to days is our arithmetic (02i), so guess."
      },
